{"conjecture": "Each locally correct change creates latent cross-module constraints—assumptions about data lifetimes, call ordering, or failure modes—that are never written down because they are invisible at the time of commitment. Over years, the constraint graph becomes dense enough that the cost of an edit is dominated by the search to discover which undocumented invariants it might violate, not by the implementation of the change itself. This explains why systems ossify even with perfect tests and perfect reviews: tests verify that old invariants hold, not that new changes are safe with respect to invariants no one knows exist."}
